High: Remove credential data from JWT payload and make auth recheck authoritative #30
Open
Open
[High: Remove credential data from JWT payload and make auth recheck authoritative](https://github.com/MAKASA-LABORATORY/BCIIS-Server/issues/30#top)#30
Description
[maxangelo987](https://github.com/maxangelo987)
opened [2w ago](https://github.com/MAKASA-LABORATORY/BCIIS-Server/issues/30#issue-5294092477)
Severity
High
Scope reviewed
BCIIS-Server — backlog branch ESTO
Finding
The authentication path signs the complete stored-procedure login row into the JWT (jwt.sign({ data: data.result0[0] }, ...)). The subsequent auth-check path explicitly reads decoded.data.password, confirming that password/credential material is being carried in the client-readable JWT payload. JWT payloads are only encoded, not confidential, so credential material must never be placed there.
The same auth-check handler sends an authenticated response before the database recheck completes, then later attempts another response after calling ST_AUTH_RECHECK. This makes the server-side recheck non-authoritative and risks double-send / headers already sent behavior.
Related auth-path quality issues observed on this branch:
/getCSRFToken calls req.csrfToken() while the CSRF middleware is commented out, so the endpoint can fail at runtime.
Several auth/database error paths return raw error objects to clients.
server.js computes process.env.PORT || 4000 but starts the app on hard-coded port 3000, which can break deployment configuration.
Required remediation
Restrict JWT claims to non-sensitive, server-authoritative identifiers/claims only (for example sub, narrowly scoped role/authorization identifiers, token/session version, jti). Never include a password, password hash, credential verifier, or the entire user row.
Perform the authoritative server/database recheck before returning authenticated user state, with exactly one response path. Consider a server-side session/token version or equivalent revocation mechanism so credential/authorization changes can invalidate prior authorization as intended.
Return generic client-safe authentication/database errors; keep detailed diagnostics only in protected server logs.
Either correctly enable/configure CSRF protection for cookie-based authenticated state changes or remove the dead token endpoint and document the chosen CSRF threat model if bearer-header authentication is used instead.
Honor the configured deployment port rather than a hard-coded listener port.
Add regression tests proving that decoded tokens contain no credential material, invalid/changed credentials cannot remain authoritatively authenticated, auth-check success/failure sends one response only, and CSRF/deployment configuration behaves as intended.
Branch naming process note
ESTO is personal/vague rather than task-oriented. Please keep existing branch history unchanged, but use task-oriented names for future work, e.g. fix/auth-token-payload, fix/auth-server-recheck, or fix/csrf-route.
No secret value or production record is reproduced in this report.
