[High][AuthZ/SP] Announcement routes trust caller identity and perform direct relational writes #33
Open
Open
[\[High\]\[AuthZ/SP\] Announcement routes trust caller identity and perform direct relational writes](https://github.com/MAKASA-LABORATORY/BCIIS-Server/issues/33#top)#33
Description
[maxangelo987](https://github.com/maxangelo987)
opened [2w ago](https://github.com/MAKASA-LABORATORY/BCIIS-Server/issues/33#issue-5313762939)
Backlog substantive audit of main found a distinct announcement-module security/database boundary issue.
Verified findings
Announcement feed and unread-count handlers trust userId and role supplied by query parameters instead of deriving identity/role from a verified server-side principal.
/announcements/admin/all, /create, PUT /:id, and DELETE /:id show no route-level authentication/role guard in this router. /create also trusts adminId/adminName from the request body, allowing spoofed attribution if the router is reachable as mounted.
Runtime relational business operations use direct SQL. This includes direct INSERT into announcements and notifications, INSERT IGNORE into announcement_reads, UPDATE announcements, DELETE FROM announcements, plus direct SELECTs. Parameterization helps injection safety but still violates the MAKASA stored-procedure-only runtime relational policy. Direct writes are High severity; direct reads are at least Medium.
Multiple 500 responses return raw err.message to clients.
Required remediation
Require server-verifiable authentication on announcement routes and enforce admin authorization for list-all/create/update/delete operations.
Derive user/admin identity and role from the authenticated principal; do not trust userId, role, adminId, or adminName as caller-authoritative identity fields.
Migrate all runtime announcement/notification/read-tracking relational operations to named, parameterized stored procedures. Preserve transaction boundaries for publish + notification writes.
Return sanitized production errors while retaining detailed diagnostics only in protected server logs.
Add negative tests for anonymous access, wrong-role access, forged identity/role, cross-user read-state manipulation, and unauthorized create/update/delete.
Suggested DSA course task
W2 — ADTs/data flow/backend operations: document and implement the request → authenticated principal → authorization → validation → parameterized stored procedure → sanitized response flow for the announcement module. Expected evidence: commit(s), route tests, SP calls, and negative authorization tests.
No application source or source branch was modified by this audit.


