[High][AuthZ/SP] Message routes trust caller-selected identities and fall back to direct relational SQL #37
Open
Open
[\[High\]\[AuthZ/SP\] Message routes trust caller-selected identities and fall back to direct relational SQL](https://github.com/MAKASA-LABORATORY/BCIIS-Server/issues/37#top)#37
Description
[maxangelo987](https://github.com/maxangelo987)
opened [1w ago](https://github.com/MAKASA-LABORATORY/BCIIS-Server/issues/37#issue-5360440781)
Backlog substantive audit of main (a94023cec148bf00c5d42379f5bdafc4d30485b0) found a distinct direct-messaging authorization and database-boundary issue.
Verified findings
Server/server.js mounts /messages directly, with no server-verifiable authentication middleware at the mount point.
/messages/conversations and /messages/thread trust caller-supplied userId/partnerId query parameters. /messages/send trusts caller-supplied senderId/receiverId and uses those values to resolve canonical accounts. The reviewed router does not derive sender identity from a verified authenticated principal or enforce ownership/authorization before reading or sending messages.
The stored-procedure path is not fail-closed. On SP errors, directory/conversation/thread handlers fall back to runtime direct SELECT queries; thread fallback also performs a direct UPDATE direct_messages SET is_read = 1.
insertNotification(...) performs a direct runtime INSERT INTO notifications. After sp_message_send(...), the route also directly selects the inserted message row. Under the MAKASA relational database policy, direct runtime business writes are High severity and direct business reads are at least Medium, even when parameterized.
Multiple 500 responses return raw err.message to clients.
Socket.IO room joining in server.js accepts an arbitrary caller-provided userId without visible authentication, so the real-time channel should be included in the authorization redesign rather than treated as trusted identity.
Required remediation
Require server-verifiable authentication on message HTTP routes and Socket.IO connections.
Derive the sender/current-user identity from the verified principal; treat receiver/partner IDs only as requested targets, not authorization.
Enforce conversation/thread authorization so a user can access only message data they are entitled to see.
Remove direct-SQL fallbacks. Keep runtime relational business operations behind named parameterized stored procedures, including alias resolution, directory reads, thread/conversation reads, read-state updates, notification writes, and saved-message retrieval. Fail closed when required SPs are unavailable.
Bind Socket.IO rooms to the authenticated socket principal rather than arbitrary join(userId) input.
Return generic production-safe errors while retaining diagnostics only in protected server logs.
Add negative tests for anonymous access, forged sender/user IDs, cross-user thread access, unauthorized socket room joins, SP failure behavior, and sanitized 5xx responses.
Suggested DSA course task
W2 — ADTs/data flow/backend operations; W16 — testing/evidence: document and test authenticated principal → authorization → validated target → parameterized SP → sanitized response/event, including negative authorization and SP-failure tests.
No application source, source branch, PR state, or deployment was modified by this audit.
